<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jagernath Lachmon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a>–<a href="/years/2001">2001</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jagernath Lachmon was a Surinamese politician who founded and for decades led the main party of the Hindustani (Indo-Surinamese) community, the VHP. In the 1950s and 1960s he governed in coalition with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-adolf-pengel">Johan Adolf Pengel</a>, and he opposed rapid independence in the 1970s. He also served several terms as chairman of the Surinamese parliament.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jagernath-lachmon.jpg" alt="Jagernath Lachmon" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bert Verhoeff for Anefo · CC BY-SA 3.0 nl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jagernath_Lachmon_1975.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lachmon was born on 21 September 1916 in Corantijnpolder in the district of Nickerie, the youngest of six children of contract labourers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>. His father was a cook and his mother cut cane on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a> plantation; the family later ran a small dairy farm in Nieuw Nickerie. At thirteen he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paramaribo">Paramaribo</a> to finish his schooling and, on a teacher's advice, chose the law. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> lawyer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> de Miranda became his mentor, an experience that laid the basis for his later efforts to reconcile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a>'s ethnic groups.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He opened his own law practice in 1940 and in 1943 joined other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> in founding the association Djagaran Will. In 1947 he was among the founders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/progressive-reform-party-suriname">United Hindustani Party</a>, later renamed the Progressive Reform Party, which he chaired until his death. He won a seat in the Estates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> in 1949. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-of-suriname">National Party of Suriname</a> lost heavily in 1955, he withdrew one of his own candidates to give <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-adolf-pengel">Johan Adolf Pengel</a> a better chance. He chaired the Estates from 1964 to 1967 and from 1969 to 1973.</p><p>When Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henck-arron">Henck Arron</a> announced in February 1974 that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> would become independent at the end of 1975, Lachmon accepted independence only as a long-term aim, fearing domination of the Indians by the Afro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creoles</a> and pointing to the ethnic tensions in neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyana">Guyana</a>. He called for a referendum, which Arron refused, and opponents mocked him as Lachmon van Oranje. He was reconciled to independence six days before it came on 25 November 1975, helped by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-ferrier">Johan Ferrier</a>'s commitment to serve as the first president.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the Sergeants' Coup of February 1980 he followed the idea of bending like a reed, which he admired in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>. His efforts at reconciliation helped make the elections of 1987 possible, after which his party joined the government and he chaired the National Assembly, five times in all between 1987 and 2001. In June 1999 the Guinness Book of Records listed him as the longest-serving parliamentarian in the world.</p><p>He died in a hotel room in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> while leading a parliamentary delegation to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> government, and was cremated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paramaribo">Paramaribo</a> on 25 October 2001. Ram Sardjoe succeeded him as party president. A statue of Lachmon stands on Independence Square in Paramaribo, and a bust was placed in Nieuw-Nickerie in 2012.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jagernath Lachmon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Charter for the Kingdom of the Netherlands of 1954 made Suriname an autonomous country within the kingdom, with its own government and parliament, in which parties largely followed ethnic lines, such as the Creole-based NPS of Johan Adolf Pengel and the Hindustani-based VHP of <b>Jagernath Lachmon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/suriname">Suriname · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nieuw Nickerie. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=5.93,-56.97&amp;year=1971" aria-label="Nieuw Nickerie on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2971 1942 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jagernath Lachmon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3421 2212h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3421 2212h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=5.93,-56.97&amp;year=1971">What was here in 1971? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
